<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Anjouan</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1912 CE (approx.) · capital Mutsamudu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Comorian sultanate, known to English sailors as Johanna, a provisioning stop on the route to India until French rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Shirazi-descended families established a sultanate on Anjouan (Ndzuwani) around 1500, with an early capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domoni">Domoni</a>. The ruling house later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutsamudu">Mutsamudu</a> on the north-west coast, which grew into a fortified stone town with a citadel overlooking its harbour.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultan governed with a council of the leading Arab and Shirazi lineages, while much of the rural population lived in dependence or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Anjouan prospered by supplying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and other European ships with water, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and fruit, and English became widely understood at court. The sultans claimed overlordship of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/colony-of-mayotte-and-dependencies">Mayotte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moheli">Moheli</a> and fought repeatedly to enforce it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Civil wars and Malagasy raids weakened the island in the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Abdallah III accepted a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1886, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the island outright in 1912. Anjouan became part of the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comoro-islands">Comoros</a> in 1975, though it attempted secession in 1997.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1500 CE – 1912 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:8.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1912 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mutsamudu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdallah I, Abdallah III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Shindzuani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/comoros">Comoros</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ndzuwani, Nzwani, Johanna</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1706&amp;focus=sultanate-of-anjouan" aria-label="Sultanate of Anjouan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5900 2631 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Anjouan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6220 2887L5054 728M6220 2887L6243 2910M6220 2887L6238 2910"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0M6243 2910h0M6238 2910h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6220 2887h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6220 2887h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mutsamudu. <a href="/atlas?year=1706&amp;focus=sultanate-of-anjouan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
